Subject: [PATCH] One classification of "what blocks a seal", not three that
 disagreed

THE LIVE GOLD BOOK PLACED NOTHING AT 10:00Z OR 14:00Z ON 2026-09-24, and the principal
noticed before the desk did. `decision_ledger.jsonl` carried 583 rows that day reading
`release_identity_refused` -- one a minute, reaching no alert, no dashboard and no human.
1,200 such rows since 2026-09-07T07:35:15Z; 19 gold sleeve-days lost against 22 placed.

THE RAIL WAS RIGHT AND ITS INPUT WAS WRONG. `release_identity.verdict()` refuses new risk
when the running tree is not the sealed one, and it was not: the box sealed dbbbbdcc4d2f at
05:53Z and every hourly re-seal afterwards was refused, while HEAD advanced 55 commits.
gold_asia placed at 05:56:30Z -- three minutes after that seal -- and nothing placed again.

WHY THE SEAL COULD NOT SUCCEED. Three copies of ONE classification were live at once, and a
seal needed all three to agree:

    dist/quant-platform.zip       known to Adopt-And-Seal.ps1, not to libs.ops.release
                                  -> pre-check passed, seal() raised: exit 4 instead of exit 3
    desks/mt5/swap_exposure.json  known to release.STATE_FILES, not to the script
                                  -> "refusing to seal", measured 15:19:41Z and 09-23 06:37Z
    desks/mt5/docs/*.md           known to NONE of them; trade_path.py rewrites it on a clock
                                  -> "refusing to seal", measured 15:19:41Z
    frontier_intel/, side_channels/  known to release, not to release_identity's mirror
                                  -> classified CODE by the verdict the GATEWAY reads

Every one of those paths is regenerated by an organ on a clock, so every disagreement was
PERMANENT. `dist/` had already been "fixed" that morning (c12e17876f, "A regenerated build
artifact was refusing every seal, forever") -- in the script only, which moved the halt one
stage later and left the commit message true.

THE FIX IS ONE AUTHORITY. `release.seal_blocking_paths()` is the single answer to "what stops
a seal"; `seal()` applies it, and Adopt-And-Seal.ps1 now ASKS it instead of keeping a private
list. `release_identity`'s mirror carries STATE_FILES and BUILD_PREFIXES, and the mirror test
pins the constants AND the classification -- it asserted prefix equality while the two lists
differed by two entries, because nothing ran it on the box.

NOTHING HERE WIDENS WHAT THE SEAL PROTECTS. Every path set aside is one an organ regenerates;
the money path, the signed judge manifest and the survivor canon are still held to their own
per-file digests, and a real code edit still blocks the seal. Measured on the box after this
change: seal blockers fell from 8 to 4, and all four are this commit's own files.

